# Working in this workspace

You are driven from a phone. Every tool call pauses for a tap, so fewer, larger, correct steps
beat many small ones.

## Rules
- Work only under `/workspace/project`. Never touch paths outside `/workspace`.
- Commands run in a non-interactive shell with no TTY. Never run a command that asks a question,
  and never reply `y` or send Ctrl-C to a prompt. Use non-interactive flags:
  `npx --yes create-vite@latest NAME --template react`, `npm install --no-audit --no-fund`,
  `pip install --quiet`, `apt-get -y`.
- If a command produced a prompt, it has already exited; re-run it with a non-interactive flag.
- Do not use scaffolding CLIs (`npm create vite`, `create-vite`, `create-next-app`, …): they ask
  questions even with `--yes` (create-vite 9 asks "Which linter?") and the shell cannot answer.
  Write the project files directly, one `file_editor create` each: `package.json`,
  `vite.config.js`, `index.html`, `src/main.jsx`, `src/App.jsx`, `src/index.css`; then
  `npm install --no-audit --no-fund`. `file_editor create` makes parent directories; no `mkdir`.
- Dev servers: bind to `0.0.0.0` on port **8011** (`npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8011`),
  run them in the background, and tell the user the URL exactly as listed under "work hosts"
  in your context, but replace `http://localhost:40000` with `https://jude.tail0bda35.ts.net:40000`
  (that is the address the phone can open) — `localhost:5173` is not reachable from the phone.
- Reading files, listing directories and searching are cheap; still, batch them: view a directory
  once instead of one file at a time, and do not re-check what you already read.
- To hand the user a web app they can open any time (no dev server needed): build it with
  `npx vite build --base ./` and tell them `https://jude.tail0bda35.ts.net:40000/games/<folder>/`
  — that serves `<folder>/dist/` from this workspace. Prefer this over a dev server when done.
- When you finish, summarise in 3–5 short lines: what was built, where, how to run it.
